          Most advice on oil stains was written with concrete in mind and then handed out for every driveway, which is how so many blacktop drives end up with a soft, pitted dip where the drip used to be. Asphalt is held together by a petroleum binder, so whatever is strong enough to dissolve motor oil can dissolve the pavement too. Concrete has the opposite problem: solvents barely bother it, but its pores drink oil deep. Below, the job is split by surface, with what works on each and how to tell whether a stain is finished or needs another round.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How to Remove Oil Stains From a Driveway: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Soak up the free oil with clay litter ground into the surface and left overnight, then scrub what remains with a cleaner matched to the surface: dish soap, a water-based degreaser or an enzyme cleaner on asphalt, and a strong alkaline degreaser on concrete. A fresh spill usually lifts in one evening plus about 30 minutes of cleaning. Never hose a fresh puddle, and never put solvents on blacktop. Set-in concrete stains need the poultice and enzyme methods in our concrete guide.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li><strong>Absorb the free oil.</strong> Tools: plain non-clumping clay litter or a granular absorbent such as Oil-Dri, and a shoe or brick to grind it in. Spread it half an inch deep and an inch past the wet edge. Time: a few hours at minimum, overnight if you can.</li>
            <li><strong>Sweep it up and bag it.</strong> Tools: broom, trash bag. Time: a few minutes. Keep oily absorbent out of storm drains and compost.</li>
            <li><strong>Scrub with the cleaner for your surface.</strong> Tools: stiff nylon brush plus dish soap, a water-based degreaser or an enzyme cleaner on asphalt, or an alkaline degreaser on concrete. Time: about 15 minutes for dish soap, 5 to 10 minutes for degreaser on asphalt, and the label dwell (typically 5 to 15 minutes) on concrete.</li>
            <li><strong>Rinse away from the street.</strong> Tools: garden hose on a fan setting, or a pressure washer with a 25- or 40-degree tip at 12 inches or more on asphalt and a 15- or 25-degree tip at 8 to 12 inches on concrete; a shop vacuum where runoff rules are strict. Time: a few minutes.</li>
            <li><strong>Judge it dry.</strong> Tools: none. Time: 24 to 48 hours. A weeks-old concrete stain usually needs 2 to 3 rounds.</li>
            <li><strong>Escalate, then seal.</strong> Tools: enzyme cleaner on asphalt (up to a week to finish working), a poultice for set-in concrete, an oil spot primer before sealing blacktop. Time: days, mostly waiting.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div id="identify" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Is Your Driveway Asphalt or Concrete, and Why Does It Matter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Check the material under the stain before you buy a cleaner, because asphalt and concrete react to the same products in opposite ways. Plenty of driveways are a mix. Older subdivisions often have a concrete apron at the street with an asphalt run up to the garage, and many homes have a concrete garage slab that meets a blacktop driveway right at the door. Those transitions are exactly where cars stop, so they are exactly where oil collects.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How can you tell the two apart?</h3>

          <p>
            Asphalt is black or dark gray, gives a little on a hot day and shows bare stone once it has weathered for a few years. Concrete is light gray and rigid, with either a broom texture or a smooth troweled face. Stamped or colored concrete still behaves like concrete. Brick and concrete pavers form a group of their own, because the joints between units hold sand that can wick oil and that washes out if you rinse too hard. Still unsure? Drip a little water on it: fresh concrete darkens as it soaks the water in, while sealed asphalt beads it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why can oil damage asphalt but only stain concrete?</h3>

          <p>
            It comes down to chemistry. Asphalt pavement is stone glued together with bitumen, a heavy petroleum product, and motor oil, gasoline and solvent-based degreasers are all close relatives of that glue, so they soften it. A spill left on blacktop for a week does more than discolor it; it can leave a spot that stays tacky and crumbles under a tire. Concrete is a mineral surface full of tiny capillary pores. Ordinary amounts of oil do it no structural harm, but the oil travels down and outward through those pores, and that migration is what makes old concrete stains so hard to shift.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Main risk from oil</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Safe cleaner types</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Avoid</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Asphalt</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Binder softens, surface ravels</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Absorbents, dish soap, water-based degreasers, enzyme cleaners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Solvent degreasers, gasoline, acetone, hard wire brushing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bare concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Deep, spreading discoloration</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Absorbents, alkaline degreasers, poultices, enzyme cleaners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Muriatic acid on oil, bleach as a degreaser</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sealed concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stain sits in or on the sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mild degreaser, soft brush</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strong solvents that lift acrylic sealer</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pavers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Oil wicks into joints and edges</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Paver-safe degreasers, poultices</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pressure washing out joint sand</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is the stain fresh or set in?</h3>

          <p>
            Once you know the surface, look at the stain's age. A wet, glossy spot is fresh and mostly sitting on top. A dull, dark patch that no longer feels slick is old and has worked its way into the material. Fresh stains are a cleanup job. Old stains on concrete are closer to a restoration project, which is why they have their own guide on <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">how to get oil out of concrete</a> with poultices and enzymes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="first-hour" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Should You Do in the First Hour After an Oil Spill?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cover it with a dry absorbent, grind that in and leave it, because how much oil you pull up before it soaks in decides whether the stain vanishes completely. On concrete, the first 30 to 60 minutes settle most of the outcome. On asphalt, speed also limits how much binder the oil gets to soften. Whatever you do, do not hose the spill. Water spreads oil over a wider area and drives it into cracks and toward the storm drain, and in most places sending automotive fluids down that drain is illegal.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which absorbent works best?</h3>

          <p>
            Clay-based kitty litter is the classic choice because it is cheap and sold everywhere; buy the plain, non-clumping, unscented kind. Purpose-made granular absorbents such as Oil-Dri work the same way and pull oil out of pores a little more aggressively. Sawdust, cornmeal and baking soda will do in a pinch, though each absorbs less per pound. Spread a layer about half an inch deep and carry it an inch past the edge of the wet area.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why grind the absorbent in?</h3>

          <p>
            This is the step most people skimp on. Grinding with a shoe or a brick crushes the granules into finer particles, which multiplies their surface area and lets them reach down into the texture of the pavement. On asphalt, twist rather than scrape so you do not loosen surface stone. Leave the absorbent for at least a few hours, overnight if possible, then sweep it into a bag. Oily absorbent does not belong in a storm drain or compost pile. Most municipalities accept it in household trash when the oil content is small, but check your local hazardous waste rules.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE FIRST-HOUR RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Soak it up, then clean it, then rinse it.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Any cleaner does better on a stain that has already given up its loose oil. Degreaser poured onto a puddle just emulsifies the puddle and spreads it around. Lift the free oil with absorbent first, then go after what is left in the surface.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Only have dish soap on hand?</h3>

          <p>
            That is a perfectly reasonable second step once the absorbent is up. Squeeze a generous line of grease-cutting dish soap onto the spot, add a splash of water, scrub with a stiff nylon brush for a couple of minutes and let it sit for 15 minutes. Rinse with a bucket rather than a hose at full blast. On a fresh spill, absorbent followed by soap often leaves nothing to see on either surface. If a shadow shows up once it dries, move on to the surface-specific steps below.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-900 mb-3">SAFETY</p>
            <ul class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
              <li>Wear the gloves and eye protection the degreaser label calls for, and keep cleaners off skin and out of eyes.</li>
              <li>Never pour gasoline on a stain or try to burn oil off. It is dangerous and it destroys asphalt.</li>
              <li>Keep runoff out of the storm drain: rinse toward lawn or gravel where allowed, or collect it with a wet vacuum.</li>
              <li>With gasoline spills, ventilate, keep ignition sources away and never use a vacuum on the fuel.</li>
              <li>Keep pets and children away from coolant until the area is clean and dry; it is sweet tasting and toxic.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="asphalt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Do You Get Oil Off an Asphalt Driveway Without Damaging It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use only water-based cleaners, such as dish soap, water-based degreasers and enzyme cleaners, and keep contact times to what the label says, so the oil lifts while the binder stays put. That rules out anything sold as a solvent degreaser, citrus solvent, mineral spirits, brake cleaner or carburetor cleaner. It also rules out the old trick of pouring gasoline on a stain and burning it off, which is dangerous and ruins the surface. Most water-based degreasers state whether they are safe for asphalt; if the label mentions only concrete, test it on an out-of-the-way corner first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What are the steps on blacktop?</h3>

          <p>
            Once the absorbent has done its work, dampen the area lightly so the cleaner does not soak straight into dry pavement. Apply a water-based degreaser, a microbial cleaner or, if that is what you have, a strong dish soap solution. Work it in with a stiff nylon brush and moderate pressure; a wire brush or steel-bristle push broom will tear loose the fine aggregate on older asphalt. Let it dwell for 5 to 10 minutes while keeping it wet, then rinse with a garden hose on a fan setting. Repeat once or twice rather than leaving the cleaner on for a long stretch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do enzyme cleaners work on asphalt?</h3>

          <p>
            They suit blacktop well, because enzyme and microbial cleaners such as Terminator-HSD or Pour-N-Restore break hydrocarbons down without solvents. The trade-off is speed: they often need a day or more and have to stay damp while they work, but they leave the binder alone. Apply the product, keep the spot moist as the label directs and let a few days of normal weather finish the job. Owners commonly report that a faint shadow fades over the following week as the leftover oil is digested.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can you pressure wash an oil stain on asphalt?</h3>

          <p>
            On sound asphalt at moderate settings, yes; on aging blacktop, carefully. A narrow tip or a close wand blasts out fine aggregate and leaves a rough, pale patch that looks worse than the stain did. If you use one, fit a 25-degree or 40-degree tip, keep the wand at least 12 inches from the surface and keep it moving. A <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">surface cleaner attachment</a> spreads the force more evenly and is kinder than a hand wand when you clean the whole driveway.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Asphalt soft or gummy where the oil sat?</h3>

          <p>
            Push a screwdriver into the stained area. If it sinks in more easily than it does in clean pavement, or the surface feels gummy, the binder has been damaged. Clean what you can with the method above, let the patch dry and cure for several days, then apply an asphalt oil spot primer before sealing. Most driveway sealer manufacturers sell or specify one, because sealer will not bond to oily patches. If the spot is crumbling, cut it out, fill it with cold asphalt patch and seal the whole driveway once the patch has cured. Our guide to the <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">best asphalt driveway sealers</a> notes which products include a primer step.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="concrete" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Do You Remove Oil From a Concrete Driveway?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Absorb the free oil, then scrub with a concentrated alkaline degreaser and rinse hard, because concrete tolerates far stronger chemistry than asphalt. The catch is that concrete holds stains deeper. A broom-finished driveway offers more texture for oil to hide in, and older concrete that has weathered away its cream layer is more porous still. Expect a fresh spill to come out completely in one or two rounds, and expect a stain that has sat for months to need repeated treatments.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which degreaser, and how do you use it?</h3>

          <p>
            Reach for a concentrated alkaline degreaser such as Oil Eater, Purple Power or Zep Driveway and Concrete Cleaner. Mix it at the strongest dilution the label allows for heavy grease, or use it neat if the directions permit. Dampen the concrete slightly, apply the cleaner and scrub hard with a stiff nylon or polypropylene brush. Concrete takes a heavier scrub than asphalt, and a nylon deck brush on a pole is the most efficient tool for it. Let it dwell for the label time, typically 5 to 15 minutes, and do not let it dry on the surface, especially in direct sun.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How should you rinse it?</h3>

          <p>
            With a pressure washer if you own one. A 15-degree or 25-degree tip held 8 to 12 inches away works well on sound concrete, and hot water makes a dramatic difference to what the rinse removes, because heat thins the oil. Most homeowner machines run cold, so a second application of degreaser usually stands in for the heat. Aim the rinse toward lawn or gravel rather than the street, and if your town is strict about runoff, catch the wash water with a shop vacuum.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Concrete Degreasing by the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">5-15 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Label dwell for most alkaline degreasers</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2-3</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Rounds a weeks-old stain usually takes</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">8-12 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Wand distance with a 15 to 25 degree tip</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">24-48 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Drying time before you judge the result</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stain gone while wet, back the next day?</h3>

          <p>
            Only judge the result once the concrete is completely dry. Wet concrete is darker all over, which hides what is left of the stain, and the shadow returns the following day. If the spot is fainter but still visible after two full rounds, switching products usually achieves more than a fourth pass with the same one. A poultice pulls oil up from deeper in the slab, and an enzyme cleaner works on what a degreaser leaves behind; the full method for those set-in stains is in our guide to <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">getting oil out of concrete</a>. The roundup of the <a href="/best-oil-stain-remover-for-concrete/">best oil stain removers for concrete</a> sorts the products by stain age.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Should you use muriatic acid or bleach?</h3>

          <p>
            No. Acid etches the cement paste and can brighten the concrete around the spot, but it does not dissolve oil, so you get a cleaner-looking ring around an unchanged stain. Bleach is similar: it lightens organic stains such as leaves and mildew and does little to petroleum. Leave both out of the oil stain kit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pavers-sealed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Clean Oil Off Pavers, Sealed or Stamped Concrete?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the mildest cleaner and step up only if you need to, because the sealer or the joint sand can be damaged before the stain is gone. A sealed surface is easier to clean in one respect and trickier in another. A good penetrating sealer slows oil down, so a fresh spill comes up more completely with absorbent and soap than it would on bare concrete. Film-forming acrylic sealers, though, which are common on stamped and colored concrete, can be softened by strong solvents and some concentrated degreasers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hazy or white patch after cleaning?</h3>

          <p>
            Then the cleaner has affected the sealer, not just the stain. On sealed or stamped concrete, begin with dish soap and a soft-bristle brush, move to a water-based degreaser at a light dilution, and only then try a stronger mix. Rinse with a garden hose, or with a pressure washer at a wide angle and a moderate distance. If the oil has crept under the sealer, as it does with old acrylic films that have worn thin, the stain will not come out until the sealer in that area is stripped, so plan on cleaning and resealing the section.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Paver edges keep re-staining?</h3>

          <p>
            Pavers bring the joint problem. Oil often runs off the face of a paver and soaks into the sand between units, and that sand then acts as a reservoir that keeps re-staining the edges. Treat both the paver and the joint with a poultice or absorbent. Keep the pressure low when you rinse so you do not wash out the polymeric or regular sand, and refill the joints once everything is dry if you lose some. A badly stained paver can simply be lifted and flipped or replaced, which is often quicker than chasing a deep stain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What protects the surface afterward?</h3>

          <p>
            A sealer, once the surface is clean and fully dry. A penetrating silane or siloxane sealer on concrete, or a dedicated <a href="/best-paver-sealer/">paver sealer</a>, buys you time to blot oil before it sets in, which turns the next drip into a five-minute job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="other-fluids" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is the Spot Transmission Fluid, Coolant, Gas or Grease?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Check the color: red or pink is usually automatic transmission or power steering fluid, a watery green, orange or pink puddle is coolant, and brown to black with a thick feel is engine oil. A dark, sticky patch near the front wheels can be gear oil or grease. Not every dark spot under a car is engine oil, and the color often points to a fault worth fixing on the car before you clean anything.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Red or pink spots?</h3>

          <p>
            Transmission fluid and power steering fluid behave like light oils, so the methods above work on them. They tend to leave a reddish tint on concrete that a degreaser lifts well.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thick, sticky or smelly spots?</h3>

          <p>
            Grease is heavier and has to be scraped off with a plastic putty knife before absorbent and degreaser can reach it. Gear oil can carry a strong sulfur smell, and the odor hangs around until the oil is gone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watery green or orange puddles?</h3>

          <p>
            Coolant needs a fast cleanup for a different reason: ethylene glycol tastes sweet and is toxic to pets and children. Absorb it, scrub with soap and water, rinse thoroughly and keep animals off the area until it is dry. It rarely leaves a permanent stain on concrete.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gasoline or brake fluid?</h3>

          <p>
            Gasoline evaporates quickly but can leave a faint ring, and it does soften asphalt. Absorb it, ventilate, keep ignition sources away and let it evaporate before washing, and never run a vacuum over it. Brake fluid in most cars is not a petroleum oil at all; it is a glycol-based fluid that can strip paint and soften some sealers. Absorb it, then clean with soap and water.
          </p>

          <p>
            If any of these leaks keep coming back, park over a drip pan or a garage mat until the car is repaired. No cleaning routine can keep pace with an active leak.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Do You Keep Oil Stains From Coming Back?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Catch the drips, seal the surface and keep a spill kit within reach, because the easiest stain to remove is the one that never soaks in. A heavy-duty drip mat or a containment mat sized for the car costs roughly $20 to $60 at the time of writing and catches the slow leaks behind most driveway stains. Put it where the engine actually sits when you park, which is often a foot or two forward of where people guess, and empty it every few weeks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does sealing the driveway help?</h3>

          <p>
            It is the long-term fix. On asphalt, a fresh coat of sealer every two to three years fills surface voids and gives oil less to grab, though oil spots still need priming before you seal over them. On concrete, a penetrating sealer reduces absorption without changing how the slab looks, and many products are rated to repel oil long enough for you to blot it up. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-seal-a-concrete-driveway/">sealing a concrete driveway</a> walks through the prep, and the asphalt side is covered in <a href="/how-to-seal-a-driveway/">how to seal a driveway</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lighter patch where you spot-cleaned?</h3>

          <p>
            That uneven look is one of the most common complaints after aggressive spot cleaning on concrete, and washing the whole surface is the cure. Before sealing, apply a general <a href="/best-driveway-cleaner/">driveway cleaner</a> with a pump sprayer and rinse with a surface cleaner, which evens out the color so the spot-cleaned areas no longer stand out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What belongs in a driveway spill kit?</h3>

          <p>
            A bag of absorbent, a stiff brush and a bottle of degreaser, kept by the garage door. Most permanent driveway stains began as spills that sat over a weekend because nothing was on hand. With supplies within reach, your response time drops to minutes, and on either surface that speed is what decides whether a spill turns into a stain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes With Driveway Oil Stains
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting solvent degreasers on blacktop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Petroleum solvents, citrus solvents and brake cleaner dissolve asphalt binder just as readily as they dissolve oil. The stain may fade while the pavement under it turns soft and starts to ravel beneath tires. On asphalt, use water-based degreasers, soap and enzyme cleaners, and look for an asphalt-safe statement on the label.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reaching for the hose first</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water does not dissolve oil; it floats it and carries it outward. Hosing a fresh puddle turns a stain the size of a dinner plate into a streak across the driveway, and it can wash oil into the street drain. Lay down absorbent and grind it in before any water touches the spot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Calling it done while the surface is wet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wet concrete darkens evenly, which masks the oil still in it. Plenty of people stop after a single round because the spot seems gone, only to find it back the next morning. Give the area a full day to dry before deciding whether it needs another treatment or a different product.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Holding the pressure washer too close</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A zero-degree or 15-degree tip a few inches from the surface can etch concrete and strip aggregate from asphalt, leaving a pale, rough patch that stands out more than the stain did. Choose a wider tip, stay a foot away on asphalt and keep the wand moving the whole time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing over an oily patch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Neither driveway sealer nor concrete sealer bonds well to oil. Coat over a stain and you often get peeling, or a dark spot bleeding back through, within a single season. Clean the spot, let it dry fully, and on asphalt brush on an oil spot primer before the sealer goes down.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does kitty litter really work on driveway oil?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It does on fresh spills, on both asphalt and concrete. Plain clay litter soaks up free oil well, particularly when you grind it in with your shoe so the granules crumble and reach into the surface texture. It does little for an old, set-in stain, because by then the oil has moved down below the surface where litter cannot get at it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is dish soap safe on an asphalt driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Grease-cutting dish soap is among the safest cleaners for blacktop, because it is water-based and leaves the asphalt binder alone. Use plenty of it, scrub with a nylon brush, give it about 15 minutes and rinse well. It handles fresh and light stains; heavier ones generally call for a water-based degreaser or an enzyme cleaner.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use the same degreaser on asphalt and concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only when the label says it is safe on asphalt. Many alkaline, water-based degreasers qualify, but solvent-based and citrus solvent products do not. If you are unsure, try a small spot near the edge of the driveway, let it dry and check for softening, tackiness or loose stone before you treat the main stain.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to get oil off a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About one evening for a fresh spill on either surface: absorbent overnight, then roughly 30 minutes of cleaning the next day. A stain that has sat on concrete for weeks may take two or three rounds spread over several days. On asphalt, an enzyme cleaner can need a week to finish, though it asks very little of you in the meantime.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will oil stains damage my asphalt permanently?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They can. Oil softens the asphalt binder, and a stain left for weeks can leave a patch that crumbles or stays tacky. Small spots that get cleaned up usually recover once the area is primed and sealed. Larger soft areas should be cut out and filled with cold patch asphalt before you seal the driveway.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to rinse oil cleaner into the street?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally not. In most areas storm drains empty straight into streams and lakes with no treatment, and many local codes ban washing automotive fluids or degreaser residue into them. Rinse onto gravel or lawn where that is allowed, or pick up the wash water with a wet vacuum and take it to your local household hazardous waste program.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I clean the whole driveway after spot cleaning?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, on concrete. Spot degreasing can leave a visibly lighter patch, because it strips years of general grime along with the oil. Washing the whole surface with a driveway cleaner and a surface cleaner attachment evens out the color. On asphalt, sealing the driveway after spot cleaning does the same job and protects the pavement as well.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

